{"causes":["The oral traditions of Norse mythology predate the written text, providing a rich cultural backdrop for the Poetic Edda's creation.","The political landscape of 13th-century Iceland, marked by chieftainships and a burgeoning literary culture, facilitated the compilation of these narratives.","The transition from paganism to Christianity in Scandinavia created a need to document and preserve pre-Christian beliefs and stories."],"effects":["The Poetic Edda significantly influenced the development of later Norse literature and poetry, establishing conventions that would shape the genre.","It fostered a resurgence of interest in Norse mythology during the Romantic period, impacting art, literature, and even popular culture.","The themes and characters from the Edda continue to inspire modern interpretations and adaptations in various media, from literature to film."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"codex-regius","sourceName":"Codex Regius","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"poetic-edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda is preserved in the medieval manuscript Codex Regius, which dates to the 13th century."},{"sourceSlug":"norse-mythology","sourceName":"Norse Mythology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"poetic-edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"Norse mythology serves as the foundational context for the narratives and themes found within the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"iceland","sourceName":"Iceland","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"poetic-edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The Poetic Edda was compiled in medieval Iceland, reflecting the unique cultural and literary environment of the region."},{"sourceSlug":"viking-age","sourceName":"Viking Age","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"poetic-edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"The cultural milieu of the Viking Age profoundly shaped the themes and narratives encapsulated in the Poetic Edda."},{"sourceSlug":"richard-wagner","sourceName":"Richard Wagner","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"poetic-edda","targetName":"Poetic Edda","context":"Richard Wagner's operas were significantly inspired by the themes and characters found in the Poetic Edda."}],"places":[{"name":"Reykjavik, Iceland","role":"Cultural Center"},{"name":"Uppsala, Sweden","role":"Historical Context"},{"name":"Oslo, Norway","role":"Cultural Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Poetic Edda","type":"Reference article","slug":"poetic-edda"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q205874","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetic_Edda"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Poetic Edda contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Poetic Edda (Old Norse: Edda; also known as the Elder Edda or Sæmundar Edda, though neither attribution is accurate) is a collection of Old Norse mythological and heroic poems preserved primarily in the Codex Regius manuscript (Konungsbók, 'King's Book'), a vellum codex written c. 1270 CE in Iceland, donated to the Danish King Frederick III in 1662 and now in the Árni Magnússon Institute for Icelandic Studies, Reykjavik. The Codex Regius contains 29 poems; additional poems from other manuscripts are conventionally included in editions of the Poetic Edda, bringing the total to approximately 34–40 poems depending on the edition. The poems are thought to have been composed between the 9th and 13th centuries CE, with some possibly preserving material of considerably greater antiquity.

The Poetic Edda falls into two groups: mythological poems (Völuspá, 'The Seeress's Prophecy' — a cosmological narrative from the creation of the world to Ragnarök and the new world after; Hávamál, 'Words of the High One' — wisdom poetry attributed to Odin; Vafþrúðnismál, Grímnismál, Skírnismál, Lokasenna, Þrymskviða, and others dealing with Norse gods — Odin, Thor, Freyr, Loki) and heroic poems (the Sigurd cycle — Reginsmál, Fáfnismál, Sigrdrífumál, Atlakviða, Atlamál, and others — dealing with the legend of Sigurd the dragon-slayer and the Nibelungen material that also underlies the German Nibelungenlied).

The Poetic Edda is the primary source for Norse mythology and the Norse heroic tradition — it contains the most detailed surviving accounts of Norse cosmology (the Nine Worlds, Yggdrasil, the creation, Ragnarök), Norse deities (Odin, Thor, Freyr, Freyja, Loki, Baldr), and the mythological and heroic narrative material that shaped Old Norse literature, skaldic poetry, and medieval Scandinavian culture.","subjects":["Norse Literature","Medieval Era","Old Norse","Norse Mythology","Epic Poetry","Icelandic Literature","Germanic Tradition","Mythology"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Poetic Edda shaped the understanding of Norse culture and mythology, influencing literature and arts across centuries, including the resurgence of interest during the 19th century that affected cultural identity in Scandinavia.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
